3. Logging Memory Periodically\
Yes, you can set up a background thread to log the application's memory usage at regular intervals. This is an excellent way to monitor for leaks over a long period.\
\
Here\'92s how to do it:\
\
Step 1: Add psutil to Your Dependencies\
psutil is a powerful library for inspecting running processes.\
\
Open your requirements.txt file and add this line:\
\
psutil\
Step 2: Add the Logger Code to main.py\
Add the following code block to the top of your main.py file. It creates a simple function that runs in the background and prints the current memory usage every hour.\
\
Python\
\
import threading\
import time\
import os\
import psutil\
\
def start_memory_logger():\
    """Starts a background thread to log memory usage every hour."""\
    \
    def log_memory():\
        process = psutil.Process(os.getpid())\
        # RSS: Resident Set Size - the non-swapped physical memory a process has used.\
        memory_mb = process.memory_info().rss / (1024 * 1024)\
        print(f"\uc0\u55358 \u56800  MEMORY LOG: Current usage: \{memory_mb:.2f\} MB")\
\
    def memory_log_worker():\
        while True:\
            log_memory()\
            time.sleep(3600) # Sleep for 1 hour (3600 seconds)\
\
    thread = threading.Thread(target=memory_log_worker, daemon=True)\
    thread.start()\
    print("\uc0\u9989  Memory logger background thread started.")\
\
# --- Start the logger when the application boots ---\
start_memory_logger()\
By placing this at the top level of your main.py, the logger will start automatically as soon as your application boots up. The daemon=True flag ensures the thread will exit when the main application does.\
