Show thinking\
No, this change will not affect or remove the agent's ability to create a narrative. That is a separate step in the process, and it will remain fully functional.\
\
It's an excellent question, and the distinction is important. Here\'92s a breakdown of the agent's two-phase process to clarify why the narrative feature is safe.\
\
## Two-Phase Process\
Phase 1: Generating the SQL Query\
This is the only phase our change affects. In this step, the agent's goal is to write a correct SQL query. To do this, it looks at your database schema\'97the table names, column names, and data types.\
\
Before our change, it also tried to look at 3 sample rows of data from each table to get more context. This was causing the timeout.\
\
After our change (sample_rows_in_table_info=False), it will only look at the schema structure. The agent still has all the necessary structural information (like column names name and vessel_type in the vessels table) to write a perfectly valid query.\
\
Phase 2: Creating the Narrative from SQL Results\
This phase happens after the SQL query from Phase 1 has been successfully executed.\
\
The agent runs the generated SQL query against your database.\
\
It receives the real, live data from the results of that query.\
\
It then uses this actual data to create the final natural language summary (the "narrative") that you see.\
\
This second phase is completely untouched by our change.\
\
Think of the agent as a chef. Our change is like telling the chef, "You don't need to taste-test every ingredient in the pantry before you write a recipe; just read the labels is fine." The chef can still write the recipe (the SQL), and they will still use the actual ingredients (the SQL results) to cook the final dish (the narrative).\
\
In short, we are only optimizing how the agent prepares to write the query, not how it uses the final results.\
\
